kosha fourier-engineering

  -- the Fourier transform as it is used in engineering (not just mathematics).
  -- every periodic signal can be represented as a sum of sinusoids.
  -- Fourier's theorem (1822): this is universally true for any reasonable signal.
  --
  -- the four forms engineers use:
  --   Fourier Series:    periodic continuous signal → discrete spectrum (harmonics).
  --   Fourier Transform: aperiodic continuous signal → continuous spectrum.
  --   DTFT:             discrete-time, aperiodic → continuous, periodic spectrum.
  --   DFT/FFT:          discrete-time, periodic → discrete spectrum. computable.
  --
  -- FFT (Fast Fourier Transform):
  --   Cooley-Tukey algorithm (1965): O(N log N) instead of O(N²).
  --   for N=1024: 1024² = 1M operations vs 1024×10 = 10K — 100× faster.
  --   enables real-time audio processing, spectrum analysis, OFDM communication.
  --
  -- frequency resolution: Δf = fs/N.
  --   1024-point FFT at 44100 Hz: Δf = 43 Hz per bin.
  --   more points → finer frequency resolution → longer time window.
  --   time-frequency uncertainty: cannot have perfect resolution in both simultaneously.
  --   this is the engineering expression of Heisenberg uncertainty at signal level.
  --
  -- windowing: applied before FFT to reduce spectral leakage.
  --   rectangular window: sharp time boundary → ringing in frequency (Gibbs).
  --   Hann, Hamming, Blackman: smooth tapering → reduced leakage.
  --
  -- spectrum analyser: real-time FFT display of a signal's frequency content.
  -- the equaliser in audio: per-band gain control on FFT bins.
  -- JPEG: 2D DCT (discrete cosine transform, real-valued FFT variant) on 8×8 blocks.
  -- MRI: k-space is the 2D Fourier domain of the image. reconstruction = inverse FFT.

